window.OSAP_FT=window.OSAP_FT||{};window.OSAP_FT["2d"]={"https://theconversation.com/who-controls-trespass-footage-heres-what-the-high-courts-answer-means-for-animals-and-press-freedom-296149":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:16Z","sha":"234d0911bd20cc3caf82074bc822c0644e3b1e320be013d999b5d1d59beaed0b","read":"https://theconversation.com/who-controls-trespass-footage-heres-what-the-high-courts-answer-means-for-animals-and-press-freedom-296149","bytes":181804,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Who controls trespass footage? Here’s what the High Court’s answer means for animals – and press freedom","by":[{"n":"Liz Hicks","r":"Lecturer in Law, The University of Melbourne"},{"n":"Lev Bromberg","r":"Lecturer, La Trobe University"}],"pub":"2026-11-11T04:18:49Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Who gets to see what happens in slaughterhouses? The answer is: officials, sometimes, but almost never the public."]},{"k":"p","s":["In a landmark case, the High Court of Australia ",["has ruled","https://www.hcourt.gov.au/sites/default/files/judgment-summaries/2026-11/hca-42-2026-11-11.pdf"]," an abattoir cannot use copyright law to control who sees video – allegedly depicting animal cruelty – that was illegally recorded on its property."]},{"k":"p","s":["But on the facts of ",["this case","https://www.hcourt.gov.au/cases-and-judgments/judgments/judgments-1998-current/farm-transparency-international-limited-v-game-meats-company-australia-pty-ltd"],", the activists who trespassed to film the video were ordered to destroy their copies."]},{"k":"p","s":["This case has broad-reaching ramifications well beyond animal welfare concerns, but the court’s verdict is complicated. Here’s what the court found and why it matters for whistleblowers, media freedom and the regulation of abattoirs across the country."]},{"k":"h","s":["Scandals exposed"]},{"k":"p","s":["Farmed animal welfare regulation in Australia is deeply ",["inadequate","https://api.worldanimalprotection.org/sites/default/files/api_2020_-_australia.pdf"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Industry bodies ",["dominate","https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=5670411"]," the creation of codes of conduct that govern slaughter. Export abattoirs are overseen by the Department of Agriculture, Fisheries and Forestry, which is also responsible for the industry’s productivity — a potential ",["conflict of interest","https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-26818-7_10"]," the Productivity Commission flagged in 2016 when it ",["recommended","https://www.pc.gov.au/inquiries-and-research/agriculture/report/"]," an independent animal welfare body."]},{"k":"p","s":["In practice, many of the biggest animal welfare scandals have been exposed by activists rather than regulators, with the former either tipping off journalists or publishing footage themselves."]},{"k":"p","s":["This includes ",["revelations surrounding live export","https://www.abc.net.au/news/2023-08-28/undercover-footage-alleged-breaches-live-export-regulations-730/102731162"],", the ",["2015 greyhound live-baiting scandal","https://www.abc.net.au/news/2015-02-16/live-baiting-expose-to-rock-greyhound-industry/6109878"],", and the ",["2023 footage","https://www.abc.net.au/news/2023-03-28/calls-for-investigation-after-pig-gassing-footage-aired/102155766"]," of pigs being gassed before slaughter, a standard industry practice."]},{"k":"p","s":["So when activists secretly filmed inside a Victorian slaughterhouse, the question of who could see that footage became a high stakes legal battle landing in the High Court."]},{"k":"h","s":["What was the case about?"]},{"k":"p","s":["The appellant, Farm Transparency International, is an animal advocacy group that seeks to “ ",["force industry transparency and educate consumers","https://www.farmtransparency.org/about"],"” about routine practices in animal agriculture."]},{"k":"p","s":["People acting for Farm Transparency trespassed seven times on Game Meats Company’s slaughterhouse in 2024 to install, and later retrieve, hidden cameras."]},{"k":"p","s":["The footage, the trial judge ",["observed","https://www.austlii.edu.au/cgi-bin/viewdoc/au/cases/cth/FCA/2024/1455.html"],", showed goats that appeared not to be unconscious when their throats were cut, and infant goats left in a bucket who bleated in distress as they were stunned."]},{"k":"p","s":["Farm Transparency argued this breached the Australian Standard requiring animals to be stunned unconscious before slaughter."]},{"k":"p","s":["Farm Transparency sent the footage to the federal Department of Agriculture with a complaint. When it received no response from the department two weeks later, it sent the footage to Channel Seven, which ran a story without showing the footage. It also posted the footage on its website."]},{"k":"p","s":["At an initial Federal Court of Australia trial, Game Meats won $130,000 in damages for trespass, but nothing more."]},{"k":"p","s":["But on appeal, the full Federal Court declared that Game Meats would hold copyright over the video through a mechanism called a “constructive trust”."]},{"k":"p","s":["This was the real contest. As Chief Justice Stephen Gageler and Justice Jacqueline Gleeson noted, Game Meats repeatedly called “control of the images” “the prize in this litigation”."]},{"k":"p","s":["Owning the copyright would let Game Meats sue anyone else who held or shared the footage — the department, Channel Seven, or members of the public."]},{"k":"p","s":["The two judges noted this would affect the public’s freedom of expression. The other judges agreed with the Human Rights Law Centre and Alliance for Journalists’ Freedom, who intervened as friends of the court (“amica curiae”). Those bodies ",["argued","https://www.hcourt.gov.au/sites/default/files/2026-02/FarmTransparency-GameMeats_Int_HRLC%26AJF.pdf"]," the copyright trust “could significantly impinge upon the freedoms and interests of third parties”."]},{"k":"p","s":["The High Court ordered Farm Transparency to delete the footage. But it refused to give the slaughterhouse ownership of the copyright, warning this could impact the free speech of people who hadn’t trespassed."]},{"k":"h","s":["What does all this mean?"]},{"k":"p","s":["On its face, the judgment leaves journalists and whistleblowers untouched. Channel Seven is free to publish. The court went out of its way to protect people who had no part in the trespass."]},{"k":"p","s":["But this flow of information depends on footage reaching third parties before a court intervenes. Here, the slaughterhouse moved fast enough so the footage never circulated widely."]},{"k":"p","s":["That matters beyond animals. Some of the most significant reforms of recent years, including abuse in ",["aged care","https://www.abc.net.au/news/2019-06-24/aged-care-royal-commission-hears-abuse-allegations-japara/11241700"]," and ",["youth detention centres","https://www.abc.net.au/news/2016-07-25/four-corners-evidence-of-kids-tear-gas-in-don-dale-prison/7656128"],", began with unauthorised disclosure of CCTV, or the use of hidden cameras."]},{"k":"p","s":["In this case, the judges pointed to the relevant regulator as the proper channel for reporting breaches of animal welfare standards."]},{"k":"p","s":["But regulators enforce rules as they stand. Much of what happens in slaughterhouses is routine, lawful practice at enormous scale. Changing it requires sustained, widespread documentation and public attention, not a single complaint."]},{"k":"p","s":["Organisations such as Farm Transparency emerge when institutions aren’t fulfilling that role. Whether industry practices meet community standards is a question only the public can answer in a democracy, but they need to be able to see it in the first place. And a picture tells a thousand words."]},{"k":"p","s":["The slaughterhouse called control of the footage “the prize” and the courts largely framed publication’s harm in commercial terms: footage of animal slaughter might turn customers away."]},{"k":"p","s":["That framing is revealing. If truthful information harms a business only because people choose differently once informed, that is not harm, but democracy working."]},{"k":"p","s":["The High Court rightly protected third parties’ free speech, but treating accurate information on a matter of public interest as a harm deserves wider debate. Where commerce and the public’s right to know collide, the public should come first."]}],"chars":5651},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/finland":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T16:38Z","sha":"b6b70e62c903359dac22aa45db3dc6ebc5b09c9180c52037fabe6712586b9ef1","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/finland","bytes":61191,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Finland travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2012-12-31T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-11-17T13:27:15+00:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/finland/email-signup"]," when this advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/travelaware/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of the current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Finland set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Finnish Embassy in the UK","https://finlandabroad.fi/web/gbr/frontpage"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["Finland follows ",["Schengen area rules","https://europa.eu/youreurope/citizens/travel/entry-exit/non-eu-nationals/index_en.htm"],". Your passport must:"]},{"k":"li","s":["have a ‘date of issue’ less than 10 years before the date you arrive – if you renewed your passport before 1 October 2018, it might have a date of issue that is more than 10 years ago, making it invalid for entry to the Schengen area"]},{"k":"li","s":["have an ‘expiry date’ at least 3 months after the day you plan to leave the Schengen area (the expiry date does not need to be within 10 years of the date of issue)"]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport if you need to","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can ",["travel without a visa to the Schengen area","https://www.gov.uk/travel-to-eu-schengen-area"],", which includes Finland, for up to 90 days in any 180-day period without a visa. This applies if you travel:"]},{"k":"li","s":["as a tourist"]},{"k":"li","s":["to visit family or friends"]},{"k":"li","s":["to attend business meetings, cultural or sports events"]},{"k":"li","s":["for short-term studies or training"]},{"k":"p","s":["The ",["requirements for working in Finland","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/travel-to-finland-for-work"]," are different."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re travelling to other Schengen countries as well, make sure your whole visit is within the 90-day visa-free limit. Visits to Schengen countries within the previous 180 days before you travel count towards your 90 days."]},{"k":"p","s":["At Finnish border control, you may also need to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["show proof of your accommodation, for example, a hotel booking confirmation or proof of address for a second home"]},{"k":"li","s":["show a return or onward ticket"]},{"k":"li","s":["prove that you have enough money for your stay – the amount varies depending on your accommodation"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Staying longer than 90 days in a 180-day period"]},{"k":"p","s":["To stay longer, you must meet the Finnish government’s entry requirements. Check which type of visa or permit you need with the ",["Finnish Embassy in the UK","https://finlandabroad.fi/web/gbr/frontpage"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re in Finland with a residence permit or long-stay visa, this does not count towards your 90-day visa-free limit."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read about ",["entry processes if you live in Finland","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/living-in-finland#passports-and-travel"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["New Schengen entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["The European Union’s (EU) new Entry/Exit System (EES) is now being implemented across the Schengen area."]},{"k":"p","s":["This means that when you travel into the Schengen area for short stays, you may need to register your biometric details, such as fingerprints and a photo. There is no cost for EES registration."]},{"k":"p","s":["On your first visit into a Schengen country, you may be asked to register your details at a special booth before proceeding to the immigration desk. Follow directions from your travel operator or the staff at your port of entry. You may also need to provide either your fingerprint or photo when you leave the Schengen area. Children aged 11 or younger will not have their fingerprints scanned but can be required to have their photo taken."]},{"k":"p","s":["You do not need to take any action before you arrive at the border on entry to the Schengen area, but EES may take each passenger extra time to complete so be prepared to wait longer than usual at the border and to allow more time for immigration controls when you depart the Schengen area. If you are departing the Schengen area by plane, allow extra time to reach your departure gate as EES checks may take place before boarding."]},{"k":"p","s":["EES is replacing the previous system of manually stamping passports when visitors arrive in the Schengen area for short stays. You may be asked to input biometric details every time you enter or exit."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you enter the Schengen area through the Port of Dover, Eurotunnel at Folkestone or Eurostar at St Pancras International, any information will be taken at the border before you leave the UK."]},{"k":"p","s":["Your digital EES record is valid for 3 years."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["information on the EU Entry/Exit System and to understand which countries are in the Schengen area","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/eu-entryexit-system"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are long-term resident in a Schengen country, you are exempt from EES in all Schengen countries. The full list of EES exemptions is on ",["the EU’s official Travel Europe website.","https://travel-europe.europa.eu/en/ees/to-whom-does-ees-not-apply"]]},{"k":"p","s":["If you believe you have been incorrectly entered into EES or your record of time spent in the Schengen area is incorrect, approach border officials when you next cross the Schengen border and request a correction. If you are exempt, carry documentation confirming your status. If you believe your time spent in the Schengen area has been recorded incorrectly, raise this with border officials and be prepared to provide supporting evidence if requested."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travelling with children"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Finland with an adult who is not a parent or legal guardian, minor children must carry a ",["letter of consent from a parent or legal guardian","https://raja.fi/en/document-required-for-a-minor-to-travel"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Russian border"]},{"k":"p","s":[["FCDO currently advises against all travel to Russia","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/russia"],". All border crossing points on the land border between Finland and Russia will remain closed until further notice. Monitor the ",["Finnish Border Guard website","https://raja.fi/en/frontpage"]," for details of border closures with Russia. Access to areas close to the border is controlled and a border zone permit issued by the Finnish Border Guard is required. Follow the instructions of the Finnish authorities if you are in this area."]},{"k":"p","s":["The border crossing points for maritime traffic at Haapasaari, the port of Nuijamaa and Santio will be closed to leisure boating until further notice."]},{"k":"p","s":["For more information on leaving Russia, see ",["Russia travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/russia/returning-to-the-uk"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["For details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Finland guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/80/finland#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about ",["goods you can take into and out of Finland","https://tulli.fi/en/frontpage"],". You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking food and drink into Finland"]},{"k":"p","s":["You cannot take ",["meat, milk or products containing them","https://ec.europa.eu/food/animals/animalproducts/personal_imports_en"]," into EU countries. There are some exceptions such as powdered baby milk, baby food and special foods or pet feed required for medical reasons."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Restrictions on Russian vehicles in Finland"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are restrictions on vehicles with Russian licence plates in Finland. See ",["Finland’s Ministry of Foreign Affairs for more information","https://um.fi/press-releases/-/asset_publisher/ued5t2wDmr1C/content/rajoituksia-venajalle-rekisteroityjen-autojen-tuontiin/35732"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking money into Finland"]},{"k":"p","s":["Declare cash or travellers cheques if the value is 10,000 euros or more. You will get a certified declaration to show you brought it in with you. If you do not, your money could be seized when you leave."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["guidance on declaring cash from Finnish Customs","https://www.suomi.fi/services/declaring-cash-when-travelling-to-or-from-eu-nations-finnish-customs/5dc6e849-7d54-4847-83bd-7094033c380c"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Finland"]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorist attacks in Finland cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"p","s":["Attacks could be indiscriminate, including in places visited by foreign nationals."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Protecting your belongings"]},{"k":"p","s":["Pickpockets may target tourists in the tourist season in crowded areas. Take precautions and keep your personal belongings, passports and money safe."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs"]},{"k":"p","s":["Illegal drugs, including cannabis, are prohibited, even in small quantities. Severe penalties apply for possessing, using, buying or smuggling illegal drugs, including when transiting through airports. You should expect long jail sentences of up to 10 years and heavy fines."]},{"k":"p","s":["Airports in Finland have excellent technology and security for detecting illegal items. This is also used to scan the baggage of transiting passengers."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Finland, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"]," and check the ",["rules of the road in the RAC’s Finland guide","https://www.rac.co.uk/drive/travel/country/finland/"],". The guide lists driving regulations and other legal requirements you need to be aware of."]},{"k":"p","s":["You need a UK driving licence to drive in Finland. Hire car companies often have stricter requirements for their customers, such as a year of driving experience, minimum age and holding an international driving permit."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check if you need a ",["UK sticker to drive your car outside the UK","https://www.gov.uk/displaying-number-plates/flags-identifiers-and-stickers"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are visiting Finland, you may be able to use your ",["Blue Badge","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/blue-badge-using-it-in-the-eu/using-a-blue-badge-in-the-european-union"]," in some circumstances. Check local parking rules."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Drink-driving laws"]},{"k":"p","s":["Drink-driving is a serious offence in Finland, and you could get a prison sentence if found guilty. The drink-drive limit in Finland is significantly lower than the limit in England."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Arctic travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["Parts of Finland are in the Arctic Circle, including some very remote areas of land. Emergency medical assistance and search and rescue are limited in these areas. See ",["Arctic travel safety advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/arctic-travel-safety-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Drone activity"]},{"k":"p","s":["Some Ukrainian drones aimed at Russia have crossed into Finnish territory. Follow official guidance as necessary."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Dial 112 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip check:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Finland","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/80/finland#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in Finland","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/80/finland"],", including insect bites and ticks."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Finland"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a list of ",["medical providers in Finland","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/medical-facilitiespractitioners-in-finland"]," where some staff will speak English."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Health insurance cards"]},{"k":"p","s":["To get medically necessary state healthcare in Finland, you need a Global Health Insurance Card (GHIC) or a European Health Insurance Card (EHIC)."]},{"k":"p","s":["The NHS’s ",["getting healthcare abroad webpage","https://www.nhs.uk/using-the-nhs/healthcare-abroad/apply-for-a-free-uk-global-health-insurance-card-ghic/"]," has details about:"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to apply for a GHIC"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to get temporary cover if you lose your card or it does not arrive in time"]},{"k":"li","s":["who qualifies for a new EHIC instead of a GHIC"]},{"k":"li","s":["what treatment counts as medically necessary"]},{"k":"p","s":["A GHIC or EHIC is not an alternative to travel insurance. You may have costs your GHIC or EHIC does not cover, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["changes to travel and accommodation bookings"]},{"k":"li","s":["additional standard costs for treatment"]},{"k":"li","s":["medical repatriation to the UK"]},{"k":"li","s":["treatment that is ruled non-urgent"]},{"k":"li","s":["private healthcare"]},{"k":"li","s":["private clinics"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is also ",["guidance on healthcare if you’re living in Finland","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/healthcare-in-finland"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Organ donation laws"]},{"k":"p","s":["In Finland, doctors may remove organs, tissues and cells to treat another person if there is no evidence the deceased would have objected. Read more ",["information on Finland’s Ministry of Justice Finlex website","https://finlex.fi/en/laki/kaannokset/2001/en20010101"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Finland"]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone 112 (ambulance, fire, police)"]},{"k":"p","s":["Download the 112 Suomi App to your phone to share live location with emergency services."]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=Finland"],", ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=Finland"]," and ",["translators and interpreters","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/translators-interpreters?country=Finland"]," in Finland"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Finland","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/finland-bereavement-packinformation"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested in Finland","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/finland-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/finland/british-embassy-helsinki"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/finland/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk information for British companies"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Overseas Business Risk service offers ",["information and advice for British companies operating in Finland","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/overseas-business-risk-finland"]," on how to manage political, economic, and business security-related risks."]}],"chars":14258,"change":"Updated emergency services contacts to include the 112 Suomi App (‘Getting help’ page)."},"https://theconversation.com/cathy-come-home-at-60-many-of-the-housing-issues-the-film-exposed-still-exist-today-293865":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:38Z","sha":"2b160e7412d5193e5b579853185b33a14121b4be5c51c46f1f9f8105df3e6def","read":"https://theconversation.com/cathy-come-home-at-60-many-of-the-housing-issues-the-film-exposed-still-exist-today-293865","bytes":179751,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Cathy Come Home at 60: many of the housing issues the film exposed still exist today","by":[{"n":"Jessica Field","r":"Research Associate, Humanitarian and Conflict Response Institute, University of Manchester"}],"pub":"2026-11-13T14:46:52Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["“You don’t care. You only pretend to care!” an exhausted, homeless Cathy shouts at a board of welfare officials just days before her children are taken from her. It’s a scene from Cathy Come Home, the 1966 BBC drama that remains – 60 years on – a touchstone for discussions on homelessness."]},{"k":"p","s":["Written by Jeremy Sandford and directed by ",["Ken Loach","https://theconversation.com/topics/ken-loach-22138"],", the television play follows Cathy and her husband Reg, who are expecting a child when Reg loses his income through injury. The couple then lose their home, the first of many instances of this. Eventually Cathy ends up, with three children in tow, at an overcrowded government hostel that doesn’t permit husbands. Cathy finally loses her children to the care system, leaving her broken and alone on a railway platform."]},{"k":"p","s":["Despite its age, the film is recognised even by people who were not yet born when it was first screened. As recently as 2016, it spawned a new initiative: ",["Homes for Cathy","https://homesforcathy.org.uk/"],", a network of housing associations and charities kickstarted to “end homelessness”."]},{"k":"p","s":["Studies have highlighted ",["controversy and debate","https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/new-theatre-quarterly/article/abs/cathy-come-home-and-accuracy-in-british-television-drama/86CD67AF7ECBF9B3CE77D8DE573D652E"]," as key to cementing the film’s cultural status. Though the main cast were actors, Loach employed groundbreaking techniques to present a composite of things that had genuinely happened to people over the preceding 18 months."]},{"k":"p","s":["He filmed in real locations, used improvised dialogue and captured authentic encounters between the couple and gatekeepers to housing – people like landlords. A narrator also listed facts about housing poverty over the visuals. One of the most damning statistics from the time hangs as text over the closing frame: “4,000 children are separated from their parents and taken into care each year because their parents are homeless.”"]},{"k":"p","s":["For decades, media and government figures argued about the veracity of the statistics, how accurate Cathy’s experience was and whether it was right to blend fact and fiction in this way. The ordinariness of fact-based drama today (things like ",["Mr Bates vs The Post Office","https://www.itv.com/watch/mr-bates-vs-the-post-office/10a0469"]," from 2024) is testament to Cathy Come Home’s experimentation with the format."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more: ",["Mr Bates vs The Post Office depicts one of the UK’s worst miscarriages of justice: here’s why so many victims didn’t speak out","https://theconversation.com/mr-bates-vs-the-post-office-depicts-one-of-the-uks-worst-miscarriages-of-justice-heres-why-so-many-victims-didnt-speak-out-220513"]]},{"k":"p","s":["The more pressing reason for its relevance is the endurance of its central theme: homelessness. Numbers of people living in temporary accommodation are unprecedented in recent times: ",["135,580 households","https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/statutory-homelessness-in-england-january-to-march-2026/statutory-homelessness-in-england-january-to-march-2026"]," in England – including 177,530 children."]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrible conditions persist, too. Temporary accommodation is often ",["“unfit for human habitation”","https://committees.parliament.uk/committee/17/housing-communities-and-local-government-committee/news/213240/housing-committee-calls-for-urgent-government-action-as-families-continue-to-live-in-unfit-temporary-accommodation/"],", as a government housing committee recently found. This can include homes riddled with damp and mould, or with broken fixtures and fittings, or safety issues. Between 2019 and 2025, temporary accommodation was listed as a contributing factor in the deaths of ",["104 children","https://sharedhealthfoundation.org.uk/publications/child-mortality-in-temporary-accommodation-report/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["The ongoing impact"]},{"k":"p","s":["The film’s enduring impact also rests on the belief that it changed things in British society. For months, newspapers covered their own “Cathy” stories. My recent work with the charity Justlife on temporary accommodation history shows the film wasn’t the first ",["spotlight on these conditions","https://justlife.org.uk/our-work/research-and-policy/lifelines"],"; homeless families had been publicly protesting about their treatment for years. But when the BBC broadcast Cathy’s experiences to 12 million viewers, so many people called in that the switchboard was overwhelmed."]},{"k":"p","s":["Housing campaign charity Shelter – coincidentally founded two weeks after Cathy Come Home aired – capitalised on the attention by presenting families like Cathy’s in campaign materials and involving actress Carol White in rallies. ",["The charity had raised","https://academic.oup.com/tcbh/article/35/3/335/7706276"]," around £50,000 by 1967 – not all due to Cathy Come Home, but it certainly helped."]},{"k":"p","s":["The film is also credited with the formation of homelessness charity Crisis in 1967. Politicians ",["even spoke of the drama","https://hansard.parliament.uk/Commons/1966-12-15/debates/c134b462-d4e3-42a0-9d17-95b57d07fca6/HousingSubsidiesBill"]," in parliament, and the minister of housing invited Loach, Sandford and producer Tony Garnett to meet him in Whitehall to discuss the issues it raised."]},{"k":"p","s":["However, there was no significant change to homelessness law until the Housing (Homeless Persons) Act in 1977 – 11 years after the film. This legally defined homelessness and gave those who met priority-need criteria and were not deemed “intentionally” homeless a right to be rehoused. The law came about primarily due to ",["civil service concerns","https://academic.oup.com/tcbh/article-abstract/24/3/424/1707895"]," about how councils were managing homelessness, as well as pressure from housing campaigners. Cathy Come Home was part of that wider story."]},{"k":"p","s":["Loach and others have credited the film with ending family separation in some areas at the time. Certainly, after a series of public meetings – one of which included Loach, Sandford and homeless families – the council in Birmingham (where parts of the drama were filmed) committed to trying to end family separation within two years. Yet a 1972 Shelter report highlighted the practice continued in the city – and elsewhere. It didn’t end there: ",["an investigation","https://www.insidehousing.co.uk/news/at-least-50-children-were-separated-from-their-parents-after-becoming-homeless-in-2022-23-85600"]," revealed that in 2022-23 at least 50 children were taken into care for the central reason of homelessness."]},{"k":"p","s":["There were issues with the film’s representation of the period, however. It has ",["been criticised","https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctvpj7hzb.13"]," for focusing on the experiences of white homeless people, even though Black and ethnic minority households were more likely to be homeless or living in poor-quality housing."]},{"k":"p","s":["These concerns remain pertinent. Today, ",["Black-led households","https://pure.hw.ac.uk/ws/portalfiles/portal/171031546/Final_Quantitative_technical_report.pdf"]," are more likely to apply for statutory homelessness support, spend more time in temporary accommodation, and are less likely to be rehoused in social housing than their white counterparts."]},{"k":"p","s":["Cathy’s family presented the nuclear ideal, too, when unmarried mothers or women fleeing relationship breakdown or domestic violence typically faced much higher barriers to accommodation. Similarly today, ",["single-parent households","https://england.shelter.org.uk/media/press_release/single-parent_families_are_almost_four_times_more_likely_to_be_homeless_or_at_imminent_risk_of_homelessness_in_england_new_shelter_research_reveals_"]," are nearly four times more likely to be homeless than two-parent families."]},{"k":"p","s":["Sixty years after Cathy Come Home first aired, many of the problems it depicted remain unresolved. Each anniversary offers a valuable opportunity to hold a mirror up to today and demand affordable, safe and secure housing for everyone. But to do that, we must pay attention to its silences and what it failed to achieve as much as what it depicted."]}],"chars":6160},"https://theconversation.com/andy-burnham-at-prime-ministers-questions-whats-he-doing-differently-295372":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:38Z","sha":"60dcdd3df79dfb0ec320640f77e670eade1c277d0601032561c796c345b451c0","read":"https://theconversation.com/andy-burnham-at-prime-ministers-questions-whats-he-doing-differently-295372","bytes":180515,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Andy Burnham at Prime Minister’s Questions: what’s he doing differently?","by":[{"n":"Lauren Martin","r":"Research Associate, University of Sheffield"}],"pub":"2026-11-03T12:24:31Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Prime Minister Andy Burnham promised from the start that he will do things differently. Whether on social care, electoral reform or ranking pub snacks with Chancellor John Healey in a video for Number 10’s social media channels, Burnham wants voters to believe he is committed to a new way of politics. In part, this means that he wants a process that is more about collaboration and problem-solving than point-scoring."]},{"k":"p","s":[["Burnham","https://theconversation.com/topics/andy-burnham-16006"]," laid out this ambition in his ",["first speech","https://www.gov.uk/government/speeches/andy-burnhams-first-speech-as-prime-minister-20-july-2026"]," as prime minister outside Downing Street. But one major challenge to this new ethos is the Punch and Judy show that is Prime Minister’s Questions (PMQs)."]},{"k":"p","s":["My research explores the dynamics between politicians, as well as between ",["politicians and the public","https://academic.oup.com/pa/article/79/1/22/8078743"],". PMQs is the big moment of theatre in the parliamentary week – the one dedicated session where the prime minister is expected to answer questions from parliament. And it is the only opportunity that most MPs have to question the prime minister directly."]},{"k":"p","s":["For his part, Burnham has also promised a ",["“People’s Major Questions”","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BBVLWF7F1vQ"]," session on social media, to answer questions from the public after PMQs."]},{"k":"p","s":["It can be an important mechanism for parliamentary scrutiny and public accountability – 82% of people in the UK ",["are aware of PMQs","https://yougov.com/en-gb/trackers/fame-and-popularity-prime-ministers-questions"],". But the event is often criticised for its noisy clashes between the prime minister and the leader of the opposition."]},{"k":"p","s":["It has come to epitomise the adversarial nature of politics in the Commons, and researchers have found that ",["impoliteness","https://doi.org/10.1177/0957926501012004003"],", ",["verbal jabs","https://doi.org/10.1177/0261927X11425034"]," and ",["personal attacks","https://doi.org/10.1177/0261927X18767472"]," are now expected in this arena. Research into broader perceptions of PMQs finds that the public ",["has a negative opinion","https://doc.ukdataservice.ac.uk/doc/7577/mrdoc/pdf/7577_tuned_in_or_turned_off_public_attitudes_to_pmqs_2014.pdf"]," of it and see it as a poor example of how to behave."]},{"k":"p","s":["Back in 2005, future prime minister David Cameron said that he was ",["fed up of","https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2005/dec/06/toryleadership2005.conservatives3"]," the “name-calling, backbiting, point-scoring and finger-pointing” at Westminster."]},{"k":"p","s":["But as Conservative leader, he soon got sucked into the rowdiness and was later called the ",["rudest prime minister","https://www.york.ac.uk/news-and-events/news/2019/research/david-cameron-rudest-prime-minister-at-pmqs/"]," at PMQs. This came after he called the then-shadow chancellor Ed Balls a “muttering idiot” (later withdrawn for unparliamentary language) and likened Ed Miliband, then leader of the opposition, ",["to Wallace","https://www.standard.co.uk/news/politics/cracking-lookalike-gromit-i-do-resemble-wallace-admits-ed-miliband-7827151.html"]," of Wallace and Gromit fame."]},{"k":"p","s":["MPs themselves recognise that ",["language in Westminster","https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-politics-49839240"]," matters. Burnham’s attempt to reshape politics comes in the context of a general coarsening of political rhetoric that has been linked to the rise of something called ",["“affective polarisation”","https://doi.org/10.1111/1475-6765.12623"]," (when disagreeing with opponents starts to become part of someone’s identity)."]},{"k":"p","s":["During the summer, the leader of the opposition, Kemi Badenoch, was criticised for describing then-education secretary ",["Bridget Phillipson","https://hansard.parliament.uk/commons/2026-06-24/debates/059CA992-8767-4A04-85D7-203364AD3FAC/Engagements"]," as a “spiteful class warrior”. She had already likened her to a ",["“Gestapo officer”","https://spectator.com/article/what-kemi-badenoch-told-tim-shipman/"]," for ending tax exemption on private schools."]},{"k":"p","s":["And Reform UK spokesman Zia Yusuf used ",["the word “traitor”","https://www.huffingtonpost.co.uk/entry/ben-wallace-zia-yusuf-conservative-party_uk_6a79e2bde4b0a81f52ff8eb0"]," to describe former defence secretary Ben Wallace over his role in the Afghan relocation scheme in 2021. It was an example of a broader toxicity in public life, that even prompted party leader Nigel Farage ",["to reject Yusuf’s choice of word","https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/politics/nigel-farage-reform-zia-yusuf-traitor-ben-wallace-row-b3031060.html"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Burnham avoids point-scoring … for now"]},{"k":"p","s":["So how is Burnham holding up in the PMQs arena? The timings of the summer and conference recesses mean that in his first 100 days Burnham faced only four PMQs. His fifth coincided with Healey’s first budget, and it appears to be a case of so far, so good."]},{"k":"p","s":["Burnham is yet to be sucked into the political point-scoring that has so often plagued PMQs. He has pointed fingers at previous Conservative governments for their failings (Liz Truss’s disastrous mini-budget, for example). But the criticism is often accompanied by promises to fix the issues and an acceptance of the challenges and a need to confront them collaboratively."]},{"k":"p","s":["This pattern has been most prevalent in his commitment to ",["reform social care","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oLlbvXNKUio"],". On this, he has repeatedly appealed to working across the floor of the House, and has noted the need to do so to restore the esteem of politicians in the minds of voters."]},{"k":"p","s":["Interestingly, Burnham has so far chosen not to attack members of other parties where his predecessors may have. At his first PMQs on September 2, as Reform UK was embroiled in various scandals about the party’s finances, Reform MP Danny Kruger asked Burnham a question about youth employment. Rather than attack Kruger over the finances, Burnham ",["answered his question","https://hansard.parliament.uk/Commons/2026-09-02/debates/64E68DAF-DE26-43C9-A6E0-ED2ED525D1FF/Engagements"]," in good faith."]},{"k":"p","s":["Burnham ",["won praise","https://theconversation.com/andy-burnhams-first-pmqs-heres-what-we-learned-from-this-bloodless-affair-291084"]," for this decision, and he used PMQs to congratulate Badenoch on her speech at the Conservative party conference."]},{"k":"p","s":["These are positive trends, which suggest that Burnham is taking his promises about PMQs seriously. In moments when he could have been sucked into the old noisy clashes, so far he has chosen not to."]},{"k":"p","s":["The jeering and jibes from the benches continue (and Burnham has done “People’s Major Questions” only once). But in many ways the respect that he and Badenoch have shown one another thus far is unusual."]},{"k":"p","s":["While it is too early to tell how long this will last – there may be many head-to-heads between now and the next general election – there are glimmers of hope for those who dislike the adversarial nature of PMQs and politics at Westminster. In an era of deep distrust in politicians, language and behaviour in parliament matter. Burnham (and Badenoch) has an opportunity to reimagine how this element of how politics at Westminster works."]}],"chars":5342},"https://theconversation.com/johnny-depp-returns-to-the-screen-as-scrooge-dickens-might-have-some-notes-295865":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:38Z","sha":"4a3d47c2838c49e6fc374d7a5c0c3bff75e22b314cd7699364f7a6c0f932a58f","read":"https://theconversation.com/johnny-depp-returns-to-the-screen-as-scrooge-dickens-might-have-some-notes-295865","bytes":181209,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Johnny Depp returns to the screen as Scrooge – Dickens might have some notes","by":[{"n":"Grace Moore","r":"Associate Professor of English, University of Otago"}],"pub":"2026-11-11T23:53:31Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Another festive season beckons, and with it another adaptation of Charles Dickens’ perennial favourite, ",["A Christmas Carol","https://www.gutenberg.org/files/46/46-h/46-h.htm"]," – this time as a vehicle for Johnny Depp’s return to family-friendly roles."]},{"k":"p","s":["Boasting an all-star cast, ",["Ebenezer","https://www.imdb.com/title/tt38773219/"]," explores the miserly Ebenezer Scrooge’s backstory. Dickens’ ghosts become zombies, and his London is a dark, violent place, filled with predators and danger."]},{"k":"p","s":["Against this backdrop, we see a different side to Scrooge: a man scarred by bullying and abuse, whose worldly ambition masks his vulnerability. It’s also a reminder of how much the focus of Dickens’ original story has shifted in the retelling, and of the author’s real intentions when writing it."]},{"k":"p","s":["An instant hit when it was first published in December 1843, the tale of Scrooge and Tiny Tim has been synonymous with Christmas ever since. It began a series of annual “Christmas books” through which Dickens sought to capitalise on his novella’s success."]},{"k":"p","s":["While always commercially savvy, Dickens had another, more pressing motive behind his story of Scrooge’s haunting and redemption: he wanted to bring about widespread radical social change."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dickens’ social conscience"]},{"k":"p","s":["Known for his commitment to social reform through novels such as Oliver Twist, which exposed the cruelty of the ",["Victorian workhouse","https://www.historic-uk.com/HistoryUK/HistoryofBritain/Victorian-Workhouse/"],", Dickens himself had experienced a brush with poverty as a child when his father was imprisoned for debt."]},{"k":"p","s":["Forced to work in a ",["blacking factory","https://www.theguardian.com/books/2023/aug/25/overrun-with-rats-charles-dickens-museum-illuminates-authors-factory-stint"]," at the age of 12 or 13, he knew the horrors of penury and the dangers the streets posed to vulnerable children."]},{"k":"p","s":["In the early 1840s, Dickens read two early drafts of reports from the Children’s Employment Commission. He was horrified by their accounts of child workers and the abuse they suffered in factories and coal mines."]},{"k":"p","s":["Around the same time, he also visited a “ ",["ragged school","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ragged_school"],"” and was appalled by the conditions in which pauper children were expected to learn."]},{"k":"p","s":["Dickens initially promised to write a pamphlet that would “appeal to the People of England on behalf of the Poor Man’s child”. A few days later, he backed away from that decision, writing to ",["Thomas Southwood Smith","https://history.rcp.ac.uk/inspiring-physicians/thomas-southwood-smith"],", a doctor and prominent advocate for sanitation reform, about another idea."]},{"k":"p","s":["The work he proposed instead would leave the reader feeling, as Dickens put it, “that a Sledge hammer has come down with twenty times the force – twenty thousand times the force – I could exert by following out my first idea”."]},{"k":"p","s":["It is widely agreed the sledgehammer he referred to was A Christmas Carol. With its focus on the Cratchit family and the sickly Tiny Tim, whose illness is probably caused by malnutrition, Dickens’ message might easily have been lost to sentiment."]},{"k":"p","s":["Yet at the centre of the story, when the mood of the Ghost of Christmas Present suddenly shifts from jovial to stern, Scrooge is introduced to a pair of children named ",["Ignorance and Want","https://victorianweb.org/art/illustration/carol/6.html"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Described as “wretched, abject, frightful, hideous, miserable”, these emaciated “ragged, scowling, wolfish” children are the product of an industrial society steeped in the greed and self-interest Scrooge embodies."]},{"k":"p","s":["When Scrooge asks what will become of the children, the Ghost echoes the miser’s callous dismissal of the poor back to him: “Are there no prisons? […] Are there no workhouses?” The Spirit then warns of the havoc the children will cause if left to fend for themselves."]},{"k":"h","s":["Today’s Dickensian world"]},{"k":"p","s":["It was a source of great frustration to Dickens that readers responded on an emotional level to his impoverished characters, yet took no action to improve conditions for their real-life counterparts."]},{"k":"p","s":["Dickens sought to shock his readers by showing them the suffering at the heart of a prosperous nation. Nearly two centuries later, sadly, his ideas still resonate."]},{"k":"p","s":["Globally, according to ",["UNICEF","https://www.unicef.org/stories/child-poverty"],", children are much more likely to live in poverty than adults. Like the real-life children Dickens encountered, their need takes different forms: lack of food, shelter or access to education and adequate health care."]},{"k":"p","s":["The number of children living in poverty worldwide is estimated at almost 900 million, of whom 412 million are identified as existing in “extreme poverty”."]},{"k":"p","s":["In Aotearoa New Zealand today, an ",["estimated 14.3 % of children","https://www.stats.govt.nz/information-releases/child-poverty-statistics-year-ended-june-2025/"]," live in material hardship. In Australia, ",["one in six children","https://bcec.edu.au/publications/child-poverty-in-australia-2025/"]," are identified as living in poverty. In the United Kingdom, an ",["estimated four million children","https://cpag.org.uk/child-poverty/poverty-facts-and-figure"]," live in similar conditions."]},{"k":"p","s":["Dickens might see other parallels, too. He believed his 19th century readers had retreated from the turmoil of public life into a more selfish, isolated existence."]},{"k":"p","s":["That is certainly reflected in the new film’s emphasis on the character of Scrooge and the causes of his misanthropy."]},{"k":"p","s":["Director Ti West’s adaptation follows earlier 20th century productions, such as Bill Murray’s updated ",["Scrooged","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scrooged"]," (1988) and Albert Finney’s ",["musical version of the A Christmas Carol","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scrooge_(1970_film)"]," (1970), which shifted the focus to Scrooge, where it has remained ever since."]},{"k":"p","s":["In fact, Victorian readers admired Scrooge’s hardworking, underpaid clerk Bob Cratchit, the father of Tiny Tim. For them, he was the story’s hero, and producers would cast leading actors in the role for theatrical adaptations."]},{"k":"p","s":["Loyal, Christian and devoted to his family, Cratchit embodied what his culture labelled the “deserving poor”. Scrooge’s visitations thrilled readers and theatre audiences, but Bob and Tiny Tim captured their hearts."]},{"k":"p","s":["For West, Bob’s story becomes secondary to his employer’s, and the film downplays the original emphasis on the Cratchit family and their virtues. Today’s preoccupation with Scrooge’s individual journey towards enlightenment is at odds with the the original story’s interest in the lives of the poor, and children in particular."]},{"k":"p","s":["Were Dickens alive today, he would probably still hold to the hope that society’s priorities can change, and that want and ignorance can be consigned to the past where they belong."]},{"k":"p","s":["Ebenezer is in cinemas now."]}],"chars":5865}};
